WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:05.590
Film Courage:&nbsp; What makes a good story?William
C. Martell:&nbsp; What makes a good story?&nbsp;

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:06.590
A good story…

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:11.780
here is the thing:&nbsp; there is this thing called
the ‘skin jump,’ did I talk about this

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:12.780
last time?&nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.780
Good.&nbsp;

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.780
I’ll talk about it this time.&nbsp;

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:21.550
A skin jump is where, we as the audience,
jump on to the screen and become the protagonist

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:22.550
in the story.&nbsp;

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:28.500
And a good story has some sort of a fantasy
(it doesn’t have to be a positive fantasy)

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.320
that we want to explore in our life, so we
skin jump and we are Elliott riding his bicycle

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.339
with E.T. and it flies.&nbsp;

00:00:36.339 --> 00:00:39.989
That is one of those moments where if you
were to turn the camera on the audience and

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.309
watch the audience they would all just be
overjoyed, because we’re all Elliott in

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:50.909
that scene for that moment!&nbsp;

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:53.949
We can become Indiana Jones and do the amazing
adventure thing.&nbsp;

00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:58.159
And we basically jump from the audience on
to the screen which means we need a character

00:00:58.159 --> 00:01:00.079
to jump into.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:04.729
A good story is about a character that fulfills
some sort of dream fulfillment.&nbsp;

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:10.270
Some sort of fantasy the audience has and
the reason why a film is a hit is because

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:15.300
it ends up hitting the dream fulfillment elements
of a lot of people.&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.810
And so if you can figure out the zeitgeist
of the audience, what people are dreaming

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.470
about right now, that is the thing that is
going to pull us on to the screen.&nbsp;

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.870
But it doesn’t have to be a positive thing.&nbsp;

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.910
I did a film noir class here (referring to
Story Expo 2015) and the film noir often jumps

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:37.480
us into people that do bad things because
we all secretly want to do bad things.&nbsp;

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:43.940
But we can’t, so….there’s a lot of movies
about career criminals because we all some

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.200
times want to break the law.&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.290
There are a lot of movies about people that
do terrible, awful things that kill people

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:58.440
that…like Horrible Bosses, that kill their
bosses because we have that ‘thing’ going

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.710
on inside of us.&nbsp;

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.590
That is the dream fulfillment of that story.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.729
So a good movie is dream fulfillment to the
audience.&nbsp;

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:13.030
The audience fantasizes about something, hopefully
as part of that audience, we fantasize about

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.519
some of those things, too.&nbsp;

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:20.099
A weird movie is where someone has their dream
fulfillment and you go ‘What kind of a sick

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:22.370
weirdo is that dream?’&nbsp;

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:28.409
And you see those sometimes and go “What’s
going on here?”

00:02:28.409 --> 00:02:33.739
A good story is going to allow the audience
to jump into that character and take that

00:02:33.739 --> 00:02:35.010
character on an adventure.&nbsp;

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.769
It can be a good adventure, it can be a bad
adventure but we’re going to go along with

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:40.450
that story.&nbsp;

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.129
So that means there is going to be a great
conflict in there some place, that means the

00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:50.530
thing that often happen is a romance of some
sort or another because even if it’s E.T.,

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:57.719
it’s a boy and his dog story, but the dog
is an alien and there is no more unconditional

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:02.349
love than from your dog or ….I don’t know
if your cat loves you?&nbsp;

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:04.669
But your dog loves you no matter what.&nbsp;

00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:09.450
So that is something we can fantasize about,
we can get into that story, we can understand

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:16.440
that story and jump into Elliott’s life
with E.T. and it’s the kid who bring the

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.379
pet home that mom doesn’t want and has to
hide it in the closet, one of those things,

00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:23.709
“oh, I found a stray dog,” but the stay
dog is an alien.&nbsp;

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:28.420
Now the dog catcher wants him and the dog
catcher’s keys and the guy from the government

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:29.420
(in E.T.).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:33.799
It’s basically finding that story and finding
a new stake on that story because that could

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:38.180
have been just a dog story but by making it
the alien were not telling the story we’ve

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.500
already heard, we’re telling a story that
is new and different, that gives us a different

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:42.989
experience than what we’ve had before.&nbsp;

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.222
I don’t know if that even answers the question?

00:03:44.222 --> 00:03:45.222
QUESTION FOR THE VIEWERS:&nbsp; If you could spend
the next 24 hours as any character and live

00:03:45.222 --> 00:03:45.230
in their world, which one would it be?

